claims=[]
def cl(n,items,cote,scans,file,lines,his,statement,status,klass,fate,refs,computation='No separate numerical computation is asserted in this passage; hypotheses and proof scope were compared with the cited result.',confidence='high',date='Established by the cited publication; no distinct first-circulation date established.'):
    claims.append(dict(id=f'CL-SA18-{45000+n}',items=[f'SA18-{45000+i}' for i in items],cote=str(cote),scans=scans,lines=[f'{file}:{lines}'],his_statement=his,statement=statement,status_on_pages=status,computation=computation,**{'class':klass},fate=fate,fate_date=date,present_status='As of 19 November2026: '+fate,refs=refs,confidence=confidence))
cl(1,[9],45,[34],P,'25–60','U consists of forms vanishing on a power of the augmentation ideal.','For an affine group G/A with finite locally free augmentation quotients, Dist(G) is their filtered A-linear dual, with convolution product dual to comultiplication.','proved there','A','SGA3 VIIA §§1–3 gives the infinitesimal-group/Hopf-algebra formalism; the finite-projectivity hypotheses control dualisation.',['AG45-SGA3VIIA'])
cl(2,[9],45,[34,35],P,'43–74','Left and right actions and their augmentation pairings.','Left/right translation coactions induce commuting convolution actions of Dist(G) on O(G); evaluating an action at the identity recovers the distribution pairing.','proved there','A','Same Hopf construction in SGA3 VIIA §§1–3; checked directly by counit and coassociativity.',['AG45-SGA3VIIA'],'Counit identity (ε⊗1)Δ=(1⊗ε)Δ=1 gives the pairing; coassociativity gives each action law; the two coactions commute.')
cl(3,[10],45,[35],P,'61–77','U_B=U_A⊗_A B under the stated assumptions.','Finite-order distributions commute with flat base change when the augmentation jets are finite locally free; taking the filtered colimit preserves this identification.','stated without proof','A','SGA3 VIIA uses these finite-free infinitesimal duals. A blanket claim without the page’s A-simple/flat hypotheses is not imported.',['AG45-SGA3VIIA'])
cl(4,[10],45,[35],P,'78–95','Proposition1: equivalent tests for X∈U_A inside U_B.','Under the page’s embedding and flatness hypotheses, X is A-valued iff all pairings with A-functions lie in A, equivalently either convolution action preserves A-functions.','proved there','A','This is the integral-lattice form of the distribution/coaction identity; SGA3 VIIA supplies the published framework.',['AG45-SGA3VIIA'],'Pairing⇒action: expand the coaction, whose coefficients are A-functions; action⇒pairing: evaluate at e. Both left and right arguments are identical with tensor factors exchanged.')
cl(5,[11],45,[36],P,'96–128','Proposition2, with inserted A-connectedness; completion square left unfinished.','Recovering an A-function f from its B-function and its integral values on all identity distributions requires separated completion at e and a connectedness argument. Only the completed local statement follows immediately from duality.','proof sketched','C','Formal-group duality is established (SGA3 VIIB §§1–2); the exact global integrality assertion with the manuscript’s undefined A-simple/A-connected conventions has not been matched.',['AG45-SGA3VIIB'],confidence='medium')
cl(6,[12],45,[38],P,'140–144','H⁰=0 gives Ad unramified; exclude nontrivial étale subgroups for immersion.','For a smooth finitely presented G over an Artinian local base, the Lie kernel of Ad is the centre of g=LieG. Vanishing gives an unramified kernel; concluding a trivial kernel needs the separate exclusion of étale subgroup schemes.','stated without proof','A','SGA3 II and VIA identify Lie kernels and infinitesimal criteria; the group-kernel qualification is essential.',['AG45-SGA3VIIA','AG45-SGA3VIB'])
cl(7,[12],45,[38],P,'145–146','H⁰=H¹=0; connected G; AutLie identity component.','Adjoint H¹=Der(g)/ad(g) measures failure of the differential of Ad to be onto. With H⁰=H¹=0 and the stated smoothness/connectedness conditions, the corresponding étale map identifies the appropriate identity component after controlling its kernel.','stated without proof','C','The differential statement is standard; the precise relative equivalence in the margin still depends on the page’s group-scheme hypotheses. SGA3 VIIA §2 and Conrad §7.1 supply the relevant published objects, not an unconditional positive-characteristic isomorphism.',['AG45-SGA3VIIA','AG45-Conrad2014'])
cl(8,[12],45,[38],P,'151','Lie Aut(g)=Der(g).','For a finite locally free Lie algebra g, tangent vectors to its automorphism functor at1 are derivations: 1+εδ preserves the bracket iff δ[x,y]=[δx,y]+[x,δy].','stated without proof','E','The infinitesimal automorphism calculation is classical; SGA3 VIIA §2 expresses the general derivation/tangent correspondence.',['AG45-SGA3VIIA'],'Expand bracket preservation modulo ε²; constant terms cancel, leaving exactly the derivation identity.')
cl(9,[13],45,[38],P,'153–158','AutLie smooth, «sauf erreur», when H²(g,g)=0.','For a finite free Lie algebra over an Artinian base, lifting an automorphism through a square-zero thickening has a bracket-defect2-cocycle; H²=0 removes it. Precise relative flatness and finite-presentation assumptions are required.','open on the pages','B','Lie deformation theory establishes this obstruction mechanism; Nijenhuis–Richardson treats rigidity over characteristic0 fields, while the square-zero calculation extends under the explicitly stated module hypotheses. The cited characteristic0 theorem alone does not prove every relative claim.',['AG45-NR1967'],'If f̃ is a linear lift, its bracket defect lies in C²(g,g⊗I); Jacobi makes it a cocycle. Replacing f̃ by f̃−δ changes it by dδ. H²=0 kills the defect; an invertible reduction lifts to an invertible matrix.',confidence='medium')
cl(10,[13,14],45,[40],P,'181–183','Rigidity from H².','For a finite-dimensional Lie algebra over a characteristic0 field, H²(g,g)=0 implies infinitesimal and formal rigidity; geometric rigidity follows in the usual variety of brackets.','stated without proof','B','Nijenhuis–Richardson, Deformations of Lie Algebra Structures, gives the established characteristic0 result. The converse is not asserted.',['AG45-NR1967'],date='Journal volume17 (1967/68); no earlier circulation date verified.')
cl(11,[14],45,[40],P,'184–188','H³ obstruction; preceding vanishing conditions.','A lift of an alternating bracket across a square-zero extension has a Jacobi3-cocycle obstruction; H³=0 permits a lift, H² controls isomorphism classes, and H¹ controls infinitesimal automorphisms modulo inner ones.','proof sketched','B','The standard Lie deformation complex supports the hierarchy; the manuscript must not be paraphrased as H³ alone implying uniqueness.',['AG45-NR1967'],'The Jacobi defect is trilinear and lands in I⊗g. Its differential vanishes by the next Jacobi identity; changing the bracket by a2-cochain changes the defect by its differential. This verifies the obstruction degrees, not a computation of these groups for all semisimple g.')
cl(12,[15],45,[40],P,'190–194','Group/Lie cohomology comparison and «suite spectrale».','A comparison needs a specified group cohomology theory, coefficients and characteristic; no unique spectral sequence is formulated here.','open on the pages','F','Rational algebraic-group, Lie and classifying-topos cohomology admit different comparisons. SGA3 VIIA and Cassou-Noguès–Chinburg–Morin–Taylor §3.7 establish adjacent constructions, not this unspecified spectral sequence.',['AG45-SGA3VIIA','AG45-CCMT2013'],confidence='low')
cl(13,[16],45,[40,42],P,'196–199;263–274','Adjoint cohomology vanishing for semisimple groups, queried without characteristic qualification.','The unrestricted positive-characteristic statement fails: for g=pgl₂/F₂, H¹(g,g) has dimension3. In characteristic0 the usual semisimple adjoint vanishing holds.','false as stated','B','Classical characteristic0 Whitehead theory has the needed restriction; the explicit modular counterexample refutes the unrestricted reading. Conrad’s positive-characteristic structure discussion is consistent with the distinction.',['AG45-Conrad2014','AG45-NR1967'],'math_checks.py/output:pgl2_F2 and pgl2_counterexample exhaust all512 matrices:64 derivations,8 inner derivations; the displayed D is outer.')
cl(14,[17],45,[42],P,'216–258','SL₂ in characteristic2: [H,X]=[H,Y]=0, [X,Y]=H.','Using H=I,X=E12,Y=E21 in sl₂/F₂ gives the Heisenberg-type bracket with central H.','proved there','E','Direct matrix commutators verify the formula; the characteristic2 exception is part of standard algebraic-group Lie theory (Conrad AppendixC).',['AG45-Conrad2014'],'math_checks.py:sl2_F2; the matrix commutator E12E21−E21E12=I in characteristic2.')
cl(15,[17],45,[42],P,'259–262','Normalizer of kY is kY+kH; kH is central.','In the displayed sl₂ characteristic2 algebra, N_g(kY)=kH⊕kY and N_g(kH)=g.','proved there','E','This is an elementary consequence of the bracket table; no separate later theorem is required.',['AG45-Conrad2014'],'math_checks.py:sl2_F2 normalizer lists exhaust all8 vectors; the formulas hold over every characteristic2 field by the same linear equations.')
cl(16,[17],45,[42],P,'263–269','The second rank-one bracket table.','For pgl₂ in characteristic2 choose h=class(E11),x=class(E12),y=class(E21); then [h,x]=x,[h,y]=y,[x,y]=0.','proved there','E','Direct matrices verify the table. It differs from sl₂ and explains the cohomology counterexample.',['AG45-Conrad2014'],'Computed by commutators modulo scalar matrices; math_checks.py:pgl2_F2 uses precisely these structure constants.')
cl(17,[17],45,[42],P,'270–274','Normalizer of kY is the full algebra in the second example.','In pgl₂/F₂ the line ky is an ideal, hence its normalizer is all of g; kh is not central.','proved there','E','Elementary bracket calculation; the final uncertain group label remains a reading issue.',['AG45-Conrad2014'],'math_checks.py:pgl2_F2 normalizer_Y has8 elements, normalizer_H has2.')
cl(18,[18],45,[38],P,'148','Construct Chevalley groups over Z from Lie rigidity.','The proposed route would require characteristic-uniform Lie deformation vanishing and control of Aut(g); neither is established here.','open on the pages','C','Split reductive groups over Z are constructed in SGA3 XXV, with root data and pinnings. This establishes the intended objects, not the specific Lie-cohomology route.',['AG45-Conrad2014','AG45-SGA3XXV'])
cl(19,[19],45,[45],P,'275–301','Forms with fixed exterior type ρ.','For a split exact automorphism sequence Inn→Aut→Out, forms with a chosen identification of their outer torsor are classified by twisted inner torsors; forgetting the identification introduces the action of outer-torsor automorphisms.','proof sketched','A','SGA3 XXIV and Conrad §7.2 give the precise nonabelian-cohomology classification.',['AG45-Conrad2014','AG45-SGA3XXIV'])
cl(20,[19,20],45,[47],P,'323–342','Split extension and its H¹ fibres.','A section of Γ→E induces a section on H¹, but a fibre over an outer class is a quotient of twisted H¹, not a group kernel with automatic injectivity.','proof sketched','A','Conrad §7.2, especially Proposition7.2.2 and Example7.2.10, matches the twisting and quotient issue.',['AG45-Conrad2014'])
cl(21,[20,21],45,[47,59],P,'343–349;571–621','Trivial neutral kernel used in testing injectivity.','Triviality of one pointed-set fibre does not imply injectivity; the assertion must be checked after every twist.','open on the pages','B','Nonabelian cohomology supplies the twisting criterion. The finite V₄⊂S₄ example verifies the logical failure independently.',['AG45-Conrad2014'],'math_checks.py:V4_into_S4_H1_R gives neutral fibre1 and a nonneutral fibre of size3.')
cl(22,[22],45,[47,59],P,'343–349;571–594','Outer-automorphism lifting asked, then «Non».','Surjectivity Aut(G)(S)→Out(G)(S) is not universal for forms; it holds for a pinned split form and can fail after twisting.','corrected by himself later','A','SGA3 XXIV and Conrad Example7.2.10 distinguish the pinned splitting from arbitrary inner forms.',['AG45-Conrad2014','AG45-SGA3XXIV'])
cl(23,[23,24],45,[49],P,'371–403','P/N, P/B, P/T and Killing pairs.','For a reductive group torsor P and appropriate stabilizers, sections of these quotients represent reductions to normalizer, Borel and torus–Borel pair data. A pair is not a full pinning.','proof sketched','A','SGA3 XXII §5 and XXIV; Conrad Theorem5.2.11 and §7.1 identify the quotient objects and added pinning data.',['AG45-Conrad2014','AG45-SGA3XXII'])
cl(24,[23,25,28],45,[49],P,'380–409','Existence of a Borel/inner isomorphism, then a local question.','The correspondence is valid as a reduction-of-structure-group statement; a global conclusion that a form with given outer type is already the chosen quasi-split form needs vanishing of the twisted Borel torsor.','proof sketched','C','Over fields the quasi-split inner form is unique (Conrad Proposition7.2.12). Over arbitrary schemes the existence of a Borel alone need not trivialise its torus torsor.',['AG45-Conrad2014'],confidence='medium')
cl(25,[25,26],45,[51],P,'433–448','Local existence/conjugacy box.','Reductive groups admit maximal tori and Borels étale-locally; maximal tori are étale-locally conjugate. Arbitrary maximal tori over a local ring need not be conjugate by base-rational points.','open on the pages','A','Conrad Theorem3.2.6, Corollary3.2.7 and Theorem5.2.11 specify the topology. Split versus anisotropic real tori refute the stronger local-ring reading.',['AG45-Conrad2014'],'Over R the two rank1 character modules have respectively trivial and sign Galois action; conjugacy would induce an impossible isomorphism of those modules.')
cl(26,[25],45,[51],P,'439–441','A semidirect decomposition of the torus normalizer is suggested.','The Weyl quotient N(T)/T need not split as a group extension. For SL₂ over a characteristic≠2 field, every representative of the nontrivial Weyl element has square −I, hence no order2 representative.','false as stated','B','SGA3’s Weyl representatives and Conrad §5.1 give local sections, not a general semidirect-product splitting. If the page intends only an adjoint or other restricted case, that scope needs an ink/context check.',['AG45-Conrad2014'],'For n(a)=[[0,a],[-a⁻¹,0]], n(a)²=−I. This checks every element of the nontrivial normalizer coset in SL₂.',confidence='medium')
cl(27,[24,25],45,[51],P,'444–447','Conjugacy of compatible Killing pairs.','For reductive groups, compatible maximal-torus/Borel pairs are étale-locally conjugate; over a split group Zariski-local conjugacy follows with the appropriate split data.','stated without proof','A','Conrad Corollaries5.2.7,5.2.13 give the precise conjugacy statement.',['AG45-Conrad2014'])
cl(28,[28,29],45,[53,55],P,'460–487;502–511','Equivalence theorem and cancelled proof; «J’en doute».','A torus alone does not determine a form of a reductive group with an embedded Borel: the based root datum and compatible action are required.','open on the pages','C','SGA3 XXIV/XXV classifies pinned forms by based root data. This is a corrected category, not a proof of the literal torus-only equivalence.',['AG45-Conrad2014','AG45-SGA3XXIV','AG45-SGA3XXV'])
cl(29,[27],45,[53],P,'488','Isom(G_m^r,G_m^r)=Z^{r²}.','Hom of split rank-r tori is Mat_r(Z); Isom is GL_r(Z).','false as stated','A','The character-lattice antiequivalence of SGA3 VIII/X gives the correction.',['AG45-SGA3VIII'],'math_checks.py:rank_one_torus_isomorphisms; integer0 is an endomorphism but not an automorphism. No reading is changed.')
cl(30,[30],45,[53,55],P,'490–491;502–511','Pic(S)=0 and the split case.','For T=G_m^r, H¹_fppf(S,T)=Pic(S)^r. Pic(S)=0 kills these torsors, but says nothing by itself about H¹ of every nonsplit torus.','stated without proof','A','SGA3 VIII/X character theory and the G_m torsor interpretation establish exactly the split reduction.',['AG45-SGA3VIII','AG45-StacksKummer'])
cl(31,[31],45,[55],P,'502–511','Tsen-field cohomology vanishing.','For a perfect field of cohomological dimension≤1, connected smooth linear groups have trivial H¹; the Tsen function-field application must respect perfection or the stronger field-specific theorem.','stated without proof','B','Steinberg’s theorem and the Grothendieck–Springer nonabelian H² result are recalled with perfect-field hypotheses in Flicker–Scheiderer–Sujatha Introduction. This does not certify every imperfect-field version in the manuscript.',['AG45-FSS1998'])
cl(32,[31],45,[55],P,'502–517','Real unitary example proposed.','Compact SU(n) and quasi-split SU(h) over R can be distinct inner forms with the same outer action; compactness distinguishes them for n≥3.','open on the pages','B','Conrad Example7.2.10 supplies this precise real unitary comparison; the manuscript’s uncertain worked instance is not identified beyond it.',['AG45-Conrad2014'])
cl(33,[32],45,[55],P,'512–517','Reduction from B to T left to check.','If B=T⋉U over an affine base and U has a split filtration by vector groups stable under twisting as needed, H¹(U)=0 and B-torsors reduce to T-torsors.','open on the pages','A','Conrad proof of Corollary5.2.13 gives the split filtration argument. General nonaffine or wound-unipotent cases require other hypotheses.',['AG45-Conrad2014'])
cl(34,[33,34],45,[57],P,'528–536','Weyl-orbit counterexample with GP₂/GL₂ uncertain.','The map from torus torsors to ambient group torsors factors through the action of Weyl representatives. Nontrivial orbits can witness noninjectivity, but the page’s named example is incomplete.','proof sketched','C','Conrad §7.2 gives the cohomological framework. No exact worked instance was recovered with the ambiguous group label.',['AG45-Conrad2014'],confidence='medium')
cl(35,[35],45,[59],P,'571–594','First table: general outer lifting false, special cases true.','The general outer-lifting implication is withdrawn. Pinned split forms split the automorphism sequence; the indicated Tsen qualifications require the exact field theorem.','corrected by himself later','A','SGA3 XXIV and Conrad §7.2 retain this distinction.',['AG45-Conrad2014','AG45-SGA3XXIV'])
cl(36,[35],45,[59],P,'595–610','Second table: Borel conjugacy and injectif|bijectif.','Neutral-fibre triviality for every twisted Borel pair is equivalent to injectivity of the H¹ map; surjectivity would additionally assert existence of Borel reductions for every torsor.','proof sketched','C','Published Borel-conjugacy theorems do not turn the uncertain injectif/bijectif word into a universal bijection. Conrad §5.2 and §7.2 fix the two different conditions.',['AG45-Conrad2014'],confidence='medium')
cl(37,[35],45,[59],P,'611–621','Third table: Cartan conjugacy false already over fields.','Rational conjugacy of maximal tori can fail even over a field, hence the associated normalizer-H¹ fibre need not be trivial.','stated without proof','B','The real split/anisotropic rank1 example verifies the claim; Conrad §3.2 distinguishes rational from étale-local conjugacy.',['AG45-Conrad2014'])
cl(38,[37],45,[62],P,'658–722','Cancelled/revised bilinear, alternating, quadratic diagrams.','With2 invertible, quadratic forms and symmetric bilinear forms correspond by polarisation; in characteristic2 polarisation has a totally singular kernel and does not give such an equivalence.','corrected by himself later','B','EKM ChaptersI–II gives the precise distinct categories. Cancelled arrow configurations remain withdrawn and not uniquely identifiable.',['AG45-EKM2008'],'math_checks.py:same_polar_different_quadratic verifies nonfaithfulness of polarisation over F₂.')
cl(39,[38],45,[64,75],P,'723–742','O(2n)/SO(2n)=Z/2.','For regular even-rank quadratic forms the Dickson invariant gives O(q)/SO(q)≅(Z/2)_S, including characteristic2, with the correct special orthogonal model.','stated without proof','A','Conrad AppendixC §§2–3 gives the smooth even-rank construction and component quotient; C45-01:75 repeats it.',['AG45-Conrad2014'])
cl(40,[38],45,[64,75],P,'733–741','Odd orthogonal quotient μ₂.','For a regular odd-rank quadratic form in the standard integral model, O(q)=μ₂×SO(q); the μ₂ factor is nonsmooth in characteristic2.','stated without proof','A','Conrad RemarkC.3.7 and preceding propositions give exactly this parity distinction.',['AG45-Conrad2014'])
cl(41,[38],45,[64],P,'741–757','Pin and Spin covers with μ₂ kernels.','The Clifford construction gives central double covers under the appropriate rank and regularity hypotheses; low ranks and characteristic2 require the integral definitions rather than real-point intuition.','stated without proof','A','Conrad AppendixC §4 supplies the exact GPin/GSpin/Spin constructions and norm kernels.',['AG45-Conrad2014'])
cl(42,[38],45,[64],P,'757–783','Centres of orthogonal/special orthogonal groups.','The familiar centre μ₂ in even orthogonal rank≥4 and trivial centre of odd SO rank≥3 must exclude rank2, where SO₂≅G_m has full torus centre.','false as stated','B','Conrad AppendixC explicitly separates n≤2 before the centre calculation. The manuscript’s unqualified table needs the same qualification.',['AG45-Conrad2014'],'Counterexample: SO(xy)≅G_m over any field of characteristic≠2, hence Z(SO₂)=G_m≠μ₂.',confidence='medium')
cl(43,[38],45,[64],P,'784–800','Characteristic2 infinitesimal equations.','The displayed λ²=1,u²=0 equations admit nontrivial dual-number points, so they cannot be analysed only on field-valued points. A full group identification needs the missing coordinate conventions.','proof sketched','C','Conrad AppendixC explains nonsmooth odd orthogonal schemes and exceptional isogenies; the local equations alone do not determine every claimed semidirect factor.',['AG45-Conrad2014'],'math_checks.py:dual_numbers gives λ=1+ε≠1 and u=ε≠0.')
cl(44,[39],45,[66,68],P,'801–839;1009–1027','Bilinear ring acting on quadratic forms.','The tensor product b⊗q is natural for b symmetric bilinear and q quadratic. It preserves nonsingularity when b is nondegenerate and q is nonsingular even-dimensional in characteristic2.','proof sketched','B','EKM(8.14), Lemma8.16 and the following paragraph establish the quadratic Witt module over the bilinear Witt ring.',['AG45-EKM2008'])
cl(45,[40],45,[66],P,'840–847','The Λ² determinant expression.','The induced exterior-square bilinear form satisfies (Λ²b)(x∧y,x∧y)=b(x,x)b(y,y)−b(x,y)².','stated without proof','E','Classical determinant construction; Bourbaki AlgebraIX and EKM’s bilinear framework are the appropriate background. The formula does not establish an unspecified λ-ring of all quadratic forms in characteristic2.',['AG45-BourbakiIX','AG45-EKM2008'],'math_checks.py:exterior_gram_determinant; direct alternating multilinearity proves the universal formula.')
cl(46,[41],45,[68],P,'943–1008','Σ/Sym/Alt/Frob diagrams.','For τ on M⊗M, norm1+τ factors through coinvariants into invariants. In characteristic2 its kernel/cokernel contain diagonal/Frobenius information; the exact identification requires flatness and definitions of every displayed functor.','open on the pages','F','Standard symmetric/divided-power functors occur in SGA3 VIIA/VIIB, but no single exact sequence matches all ambiguous symbols and arrow directions.',['AG45-SGA3VIIA','AG45-SGA3VIIB'],confidence='low')
cl(47,[42,43],45,[66,68],P,'801–904;1009–1027','Possible quadratic×quadratic product.','A natural product with pure-tensor rule Q(x⊗y)=q(x)r(y), equivariant under factor isometries, cannot exist on all quadratic forms in characteristic2.','open on the pages','B','EKM’s bilinear action is the established replacement. The stated no-go result is an independent finite counterexample to this precise set of axioms, not to every possible enrichment.',['AG45-EKM2008'],'math_checks.py:quadratic_tensor_obstruction exhausts1024 forms on F₂⁴, finds2 candidates and proves the factor swap fixes neither.')
cl(48,[44],45,[70],P,'1056–1087','Characteristic2 square-summand collapse and bigrading.','The sum x₁²+…+x_n² equals (x₁+…+x_n)², but as a quadratic space it is a one-dimensional square plus a zero radical of dimension n−1. Rank cannot be discarded in an isomorphism.','false as stated','B','EKM §10 distinguishes totally singular forms and their underlying dimensions. The undefined pair-group/bigrading remains unidentified.',['AG45-EKM2008'],'Use the invertible coordinate change z₁=Σx_i,z_j=x_j for j≥2; the form becomes z₁² and still has n coordinates.')
cl(49,[45],45,[76],P,'1104–1120','F generated by forms, with rank.','For nondegenerate forms over a characteristic≠2 field, orthogonal sum and tensor product give GW(k); the rank survives, unlike in W(k).','stated without proof','B','EKM §2 distinguishes Witt–Grothendieck and Witt rings. The manuscript’s broader ring conventions remain explicit.',['AG45-EKM2008'])
cl(50,[46],45,[76],P,'1120–1123','Discriminant invariant.','Discriminant is multiplicative on orthogonal sums before signed normalization; over a ring its cohomological Kummer class needs a unit determinant. Generic nonzero determinant is insufficient for a quadratic torsor.','stated without proof','B','EKM §§13–14 and Stacks Kummer give the correctly scoped field/unimodular statements.',['AG45-EKM2008','AG45-StacksKummer'])
cl(51,[47],45,[76],P,'1124–1126','Clifford invariant homomorphism on the rank/discriminant kernel.','On the even-rank trivial-discriminant Witt subgroup I², the Clifford invariant is additive into Br(k)[2]; unrestricted orthogonal sums require a discriminant cup-product correction.','stated without proof','B','EKM §14 and the degree2 comparison theorem establish the restricted invariant. No all-rank normalization is inferred from the uncertain F² label.',['AG45-EKM2008','AG45-CCMT2013'])
cl(52,[48],45,[76],P,'1128–1129','Stiefel–Whitney classes over a local ring of residue characteristic≠2.','For a unimodular symmetric bundle with2 invertible, étale Hasse–Witt classes exist and obey sum/splitting formulas.','stated without proof','B','Jardine’s universal classes and Cassou-Noguès–Chinburg–Morin–Taylor §§4–5 establish the scheme-level construction and normalized comparison.',['AG45-CCMT2013','AG45-EKV1993'])
cl(53,[49],45,[77],P,'1133–1139','Successive rank, discriminant, Clifford filtration.','For characteristic≠2 fields, I/I²≅k*/k*² and I²/I³≅Br(k)[2]; identifying the indicated decomposable subgroup over general rings needs separate definitions.','stated without proof','B','Milnor’s conjecture and its norm-residue/quadric developments give the field identifications; EKM §§14,16,44 provides exact statements.',['AG45-EKM2008'])
cl(54,[50],45,[77],P,'1144–1149','Characteristic-independent cohomological theory.','The literal constant-Z/2 theory cannot retain all characteristic2 quadratic information. The author’s C45-01:75 instead proposes mixed μ₂ and Z/2 coefficients.','open on the pages','C','Stacks Artin–Schreier proves affine H^i_et(Z/2)=0 for i≥2; EKM §§13–16 develops characteristic2 discriminant/Clifford invariants in different coefficient theories. A single unrestricted scheme-level ring as imagined is not identified.',['AG45-StacksAS','AG45-EKM2008'])
cl(55,[51],45,[78],P,'1157','Algebraically closed-field example.','For algebraically closed k of characteristic≠2, GW(k)≅Z by rank, while W(k)≅Z/2.','stated without proof','E','Diagonalisation and square roots classify forms; EKM §2 confirms the normalization.',['AG45-EKM2008'],'Every coefficient becomes1; a form is determined by dimension, group completion givesZ; quotient by the hyperbolic rank2 class givesZ/2.')
cl(56,[51],45,[78],P,'1158','Finite-field table.','For a finite field of odd order, rank and discriminant classify nonsingular forms; GW(k)≅Z⊕Z/2 as an abelian group and the reduced filtration ends after its discriminant term.','stated without proof','E','Classical finite-field classification, recorded in EKM’s Witt-ring examples; characteristic2 is outside this table’s normalization.',['AG45-EKM2008'],'The rank-zero generator <a>−<1> for a nonsquare has order2 since <a,a> and <1,1> have the same rank and determinant square class.')
cl(57,[51],45,[78],P,'1159','p-adic table and F³=0.','For a nonarchimedean local field of characteristic0, dimension, determinant and Hasse invariant classify forms; I³=0, and the rank-zero torsion exponent divides4.','stated without proof','E','The classical local quadratic-form classification gives exactly this finite filtration; EKM §§14,36 and the global comparison references supply the modern statement.',['AG45-EKM2008'],'No enumeration proves the p-adic theorem; low invariants and rank conventions were matched, and the asserted exponent follows from the classical Witt-group classification.')
cl(58,[51],45,[78],P,'1160','Real field: rank-zero Z, then2Z and4Z.','GW(R)≅Z² by positive and negative indices. With u=<−1>−<1>, rank-zero is Zu and its powers are2^(n−1)Zu up to signs, because u²=−2u.','stated without proof','E','Sylvester classification verifies the displayed filtration; EKM §33 treats signatures.',['AG45-EKM2008'],'Let x=<−1>, x²=1; (x−1)²=−2(x−1), hence all powers follow by induction.')
cl(59,[54],45,[78],P,'1163–1167','Henselian valued example, «discrètement» deleted.','For a henselian valued field with algebraically closed residue field of characteristic≠2, k*/k*²≅Γ/2Γ. The value group must be specified before replacing it byZ/2.','corrected by himself later','B','EKM §19 treats discrete valuations; the general square-class statement follows from Hensel lifting of units. The deletion prevents the discrete conclusion without a replacement hypothesis.',['AG45-EKM2008'],'For unit u, lift a residue square root using derivative2v invertible; valuation then induces the isomorphism modulo squares. ForΓ=Z², Γ/2Γ has4 elements, not2.')
cl(60,[52],45,[78],P,'1173–1177','Local–global injection into Σ of local groups.','Hasse–Minkowski detects a global quadratic form by all completions of a number field. For full GW, the target must allow the common nonzero rank at infinitely many places.','stated without proof','E','The classical local–global theorem is established; the manuscript’s Σ is ambiguous between product/restricted product/reduced groups.',['AG45-EKM2008'],'A rank1 global form remains rank1 at every completion, so its tuple has infinite support and is not in an ordinary direct sum.')
cl(61,[53],45,[78],P,'1184–1188','Number-field signature-zero torsion killed by4.','In W(k) for a number field, signature-zero classes are torsion and are annihilated by4; the rank-zero GW version is obtained by the rank splitting.','stated without proof','E','Classical local classification plus Hasse–Minkowski gives the exponent bound; EKM §§32–33 gives the general torsion/signature setting.',['AG45-EKM2008'],'At real places signature-zero class vanishes; at complex places4killsW; at finite places4killsW. Hasse–Minkowski injectivity gives4x=0 globally.')
cl(62,[55],45,[79],P,'1190–1201','Pic/2 term in the arithmetic diagram.','The canonical Kummer sequence yields0→Pic(S)/2→H²_fppf(S,μ₂)→H²_fppf(S,G_m)[2]→0. Use the étale site if2 is invertible.','stated without proof','A','Grothendieck’s Kummer framework, now Stacks59.28.1/3 (03PK), gives the precise map.',['AG45-StacksKummer'])
cl(63,[56],45,[79],P,'1190–1201','F′(A) versus Pic(A)/2.','The manuscript proposes a quadratic-form subgroup identification, but neither the exact domain F′ nor the arrow is sufficiently defined to match a theorem over a Dedekind ring.','open on the pages','F','Searches for Dedekind Grothendieck–Witt localization and Kummer Pic/2 identify adjacent exact sequences, not this unspecified equality. It is not declared an undeveloped theorem.',['AG45-StacksKummer','AG45-EKM2008'],confidence='low')
cl(64,[57],45,[79],P,'1231–1232','Completed DVR direct-sum endpoint.','A Jordan decomposition of quadratic lattices by valuation can motivate a graded direct sum of residue-form groups when2 is invertible; it is not the same as the usual two-summand Witt decomposition of a fraction field.','stated without proof','F','EKM §19 establishes the latter under precise discrete/henselian hypotheses. The manuscript’s E′/Eᵈ, allowed valuations and degeneracy conventions are insufficient for the former exact formula.',['AG45-EKM2008'],confidence='low')
cl(65,[58],45,[79],P,'1212–1229','Cancelled idelic/tori diagram.','The cancelled sketch suggests a local–global cohomological description of quadratic-form data but supplies no defined complete exact sequence.','corrected by himself later','F','Class-field and quadratic-form localization searches did not identify a theorem with these exact undefined terms; the sketch remains withdrawn.',['AG45-EKM2008'],confidence='low')
cl(66,[59],45,[33],P,'2–24','Ordered-ray coordinates and determinant.','For v(t)=(1−t²,2t), det(v(t₁),v(t₂))=2(t₂−t₁)(1+t₁t₂). Deducing a global ordered-ray comparison requires the missing interval/sign restrictions.','proved there','E','Elementary projective-line parameterisation; the absent preceding proposition prevents identifying the requested counterexample.',['AG45-BourbakiIX'],'math_checks.py:ordered_ray_determinant verifies the polynomial identity exactly.')
cl(67,[60],45,[39,41],P,'159–171;201–215','Local rings inside the function field determine specialisations and points.','For an integral separated scheme, compatible inclusions of local rings in the common function field detect specialization; separation prevents distinct centres of the same valuation. The printed noetherian restrictions are retained.','proof sketched','A','EGAI §8 develops rational maps and local-ring reconstruction for integral schemes. This is reused typescript, not securely dated new manuscript mathematics.',['AG45-EGAI'])
cl(68,[60],45,[39,41],P,'168–176;203–211','Recover topology/sheaf; dominant morphism from local ring data.','For a dominant rational map between integral schemes, regularity at a point is equivalent to a suitable local-ring inclusion; compatible local maps glue uniquely when the target is separated.','proof sketched','A','EGAI §8 gives the exact published setting; the blank intersection symbol is not filled from memory.',['AG45-EGAI'])
cl(69,[60],45,[39],P,'178–180','Separated locally immersive map from irreducible X is an immersion.','The partially written statement needs the intended meaning of locally immersive, finiteness and topology. Pointwise local immersions alone are not silently promoted to a global immersion.','proof sketched','F','EGAI immersion/rational-map criteria provide adjacent results; the truncated exact assertion could not be uniquely matched.',['AG45-EGAI'],confidence='low')
cl(70,[61],45,[46],P,'302–322','Proposition1 and the locally finite/star-refinement lemma.','On a paracompact Hausdorff space, an open cover has a locally finite refinement with suitable closures/star refinement; these permit extension and gluing of sections of a soft sheaf. The page’s PFII convention is not supplied.','proof sketched','E','Classical paracompact-space and soft-sheaf theory, treated in Godement1958; the exact PFII shorthand remains a reading/context limit.',['AG45-Godement1958'])
cl(71,[61],45,[48],P,'350–357','Sheaf of smooth functions is soft.','On a paracompact smooth manifold, smooth functions defined near a closed set extend globally by a bump-function partition of unity.','stated without proof','E','Classical smooth partitions of unity establish the claim; Godement’s soft-sheaf formulation supplies the historical comparison.',['AG45-Godement1958'],'Choose a locally finite subordinate smooth partition, extend each supported local product by zero, and sum; near the closed set the sum reproduces the original section.')
cl(72,[61],45,[48],P,'358–363','A quotient of a soft sheaf of rings is soft.','For an ideal quotient of a soft sheaf of rings under the page’s paracompact hypotheses, local lifts can be patched using partitions of unity in the ring; arbitrary quotient sheaves without this ring/partition structure are not included.','proved there','E','Godement1958 soft-sheaf methods give the background; the ring-module structure is the reason the gluing works.',['AG45-Godement1958'])
cl(73,[61],45,[48],P,'364–365','Locally finite sums of sections.','A locally finite family of sheaf sections can be added because only finitely many summands occur on each sufficiently small neighbourhood; the results glue by the sheaf axiom.','proved there','E','Elementary sheaf algebra, standard before this draft.',['AG45-Godement1958'],'At each point choose a neighbourhood meeting finitely many supports; finite sums agree on overlaps, hence glue uniquely.')
cl(74,[61],45,[48],P,'366–370','Partitions of unity in the ring sheaf.','Sections subordinate to a locally finite cover with sum1 supply the patching operators used by the preceding soft-module argument.','stated without proof','E','Classical partition-of-unity formalism; no étale/fppf analogue is asserted.',['AG45-Godement1958'])
cl(75,[62],45,[50,52,54,56],P,'412–432;449–459;492–501;518–527','Proposition3: maximal isotropic spaces, one finite-dimensional.','For the stated nondegenerate symmetric/alternating/hermitian setting, if one maximal totally isotropic subspace is finite-dimensional, the comparison argument bounds the other and gives equal Witt index under the page’s hypotheses. The ambient space need not be finite-dimensional.','proved there','E','Witt’s decomposition theory predates the draft; EKM Theorems8.3/8.5 give finite-dimensional quadratic versions. The source’s broader finite-isotropic argument was checked on its own terms.',['AG45-EKM2008','AG45-BourbakiIX'])
cl(76,[62],45,[52,54,56],P,'449–459;492–527','Isotropic complement and Witt decomposition.','A finite-dimensional nondegenerate summand splits orthogonally. A finite totally isotropic subspace in a nonsingular space can be paired with an isotropic complement to form hyperbolic planes, under the form/characteristic conventions stated.','proved there','E','Witt decomposition is established before1961; EKM §8 gives precise modern quadratic statements.',['AG45-EKM2008','AG45-BourbakiIX'],'The restriction pairing identifies a complementary dual space; adjusting its Gram matrix kills self-pairing when allowed, then split off its nondegenerate hyperbolic span. Characteristic2 quadratic forms require the quadratic rather than mere symmetric-bilinear version.')
cl(77,[62],45,[54],P,'492–501','Zorn maximality and orthogonal splitting.','Totally isotropic subspaces have maximal extensions by unions of chains; finite nondegenerate subspaces split as E=F⊕F⊥.','proved there','E','Elementary linear algebra underlying Witt’s theorem.',['AG45-BourbakiIX','AG45-EKM2008'],'A chain union remains isotropic because every finite set of its vectors lies in one member. For finite nonsingular F, solve its Gram linear system to subtract the F-component of every vector.')
cl(78,[63],45,[58,60],P,'537–570;622–638','Real and imaginary parts of a hermitian form; equations3–11.','For A=K[i] free of rank2 and conjugation i↦−i, sesquilinear forms correspond to j,j′-invariant K-bilinear real parts; imaginary parts give the analogous correspondence with a minus sign in the second-variable formula.','proved there','E','Classical hermitian linear algebra; Bourbaki AlgebraIX is the identifiable reused text family.',['AG45-BourbakiIX'],'Substitute Φ=B+iC; A-linearity in the first variable and conjugate-linearity in the second give B(jx,y)=−C(x,y), B(x,j′y)=C(x,y). Thus Φ=B+iB(x,j′y)=−C(x,j′y)+iC.')
cl(79,[63],45,[60],P,'639–645','Equation12 has a plus before Φ₂(x,j′x′).','With the convention used in equations8–11, equation12 requires a minus. The literal plus formula fails for Φ(z,w)=z conjugate(w) over C/R.','false as stated','E','The corrected identity is elementary and agrees with the earlier equation8. This is an ink alarm, not a decision whether the original author or transcription introduced the sign.',['AG45-BourbakiIX'],'math_checks.py:hermitian_sign evaluates x=x′=1: trueΦ=1, plus reconstruction=−1, minus reconstruction=1.')
cl(80,[65],45,[67],P,'905–919','Contraction associativity equation31.','For finite projective modules, defining interior contraction by the perfect exterior pairing gives the usual composition rule with order/sign determined by the chosen left-contraction convention.','proved there','E','Classical exterior algebra; the page’s own pairing definition fixes the convention.',['AG45-BourbakiIII'],'Pair both composite operators with an arbitrary exterior dual element and use associativity of wedge multiplication; perfectness makes equality of pairings equality of operators.')
cl(81,[65],45,[67],P,'920–934','Functoriality equations32–33.','Exterior contraction commutes with transport by an isomorphism and is natural for a map together with the corresponding transpose, in the typed degrees stated.','proved there','E','Classical exterior duality; a missing transpose would make the formula ill-typed.',['AG45-BourbakiIII'],'Pair with a dual wedge and move the linear map through the pairing using its transpose; the two sides coincide by definition.')
cl(82,[65],45,[67],P,'935–942','Equation34 deferred to §6 n°4.','Contraction of a decomposable q-vector by a decomposable p-covector is the signed sum over p-element subsets of pairings times the complementary wedge.','stated without proof','E','The shuffle formula is classical; its source proof is explicitly deferred.',['AG45-BourbakiIII'],'Expand the determinant pairing multilinearly: each choice of p columns contributes the corresponding minor, with sign of the shuffle. The complement must be in{1,…,q}, and contraction degree is q−p.')
cl(83,[64],45,[69],P,'1032–1036','Pairing characterization need not be unique.','Without projectivity/perfect pairing, equal pairings with all dual elements need not identify vectors or contractions.','stated without proof','E','Elementary module-duality phenomenon; finite projectivity is the standard sufficient repair.',['AG45-BourbakiIII'],'math_checks.py:contraction_nonuniqueness uses M=Z/2, whose Z-dual is0.')
cl(84,[65],45,[69],P,'1038–1044','Canonical coproduct construction.','The exterior coproduct Δ(v)=v⊗1+1⊗v, with graded tensor multiplication, gives a natural sum over all ordered subset decompositions.','proof sketched','E','Classical graded Hopf exterior algebra; the degree and complement indices must be correctly transcribed.',['AG45-BourbakiIII'],'Expand ∏(v_i⊗1+1⊗v_i); crossing odd-degree terms supplies the shuffle sign, yielding each subset once.')
cl(85,[65],45,[69],P,'1046–1055','Equations36–37 on contractions.','Contraction formulas obtained from the exterior coproduct satisfy the displayed signed composition identity when the dual argument and source/target degrees are corrected as necessary.','proof sketched','E','The exterior-Hopf construction proves the standard identity; uncertain stars and indices remain readings to inspect.',['AG45-BourbakiIII'],'The coproduct is coassociative; evaluating its first factors against covectors gives the composition law. Graded interchange supplies the sign. This is a general derivation, not a random matrix test.',confidence='medium')
cl(86,[65],45,[71],P,'1088–1103','Minors formula and Proposition5.','The coordinates of x₁∧…∧x_p in the increasing wedge basis are the p×p minors of the matrix whose columns are x_j. The transcribed xi_ji index needs checking.','proved there','E','Classical multilinear determinant theory; Bourbaki AlgebraIII is the reused source family. No determinant-of-an-exterior-power assertion is present on this page.',['AG45-BourbakiIII'],'Expand x₁∧…∧x_p by multilinearity. Repeated indices vanish; collecting each increasing p-element index set leaves the alternating sum over permutations, which is precisely its minor.')
cl(87,[65],45,[65],C/'C45-01_transcription.tex','73–100','Laplace expansion from Λ^p u.','The determinant expansion along p selected rows is the sum of complementary minors with their shuffle signs.','proved there','E','Classical Laplace expansion; matches the exterior-minor computation on Pro c45:71.',['AG45-BourbakiIII'],'Expand the top wedge as the product of degreep and degree(n−p) wedges; only complementary column sets survive, each with its permutation sign.')
cl(88,[65],45,[80],C/'C45-01_transcription.tex','192–218','Λ(M*) is the graded dual of Λ(M).','For finite projective M, the determinant pairing identifies Λ^p(M*) with(Λ^pM)* and respects transpose; finite rank makes the direct-sum graded dual unambiguous.','proved there','E','Classical exterior duality. For arbitrary modules, the preceding nonuniqueness warning blocks an unconditional extension.',['AG45-BourbakiIII'],'On a local basis the pairing matrix between increasing wedge bases is the identity; local isomorphisms glue. Transpose compatibility follows from the determinant pairing.')

